 suits, winds, dragons. I have W3 pung (W suit), hand has T tiles (T suit), B3 (B suit), and honors F4, J1. So I have all types already. All Types pattern is 6 points. Plus Dragon Pung (J2) 2 points = 8 points. That could be a winning hand if I complete it. But need to form two more melds and a pair from my hand tiles.\n\nAll Types: contains tiles of all three suits, wind tiles, and dragon tiles. My current set: W3 pung (W suit), J2 pung (dragon), hand: T2, T3, T3, T5, T8 (T suit), B3 (B suit), F4 (wind), J1 (dragon). So yes, already have all types. But for the pattern to count, the final winning hand must contain all types. So if I can complete a standard hand with these, I get 6 points for All Types, plus 2 for Dragon Pung (J2), total 8 points, which is the minimum to win. Also possibly other small patterns like Pung of Terminals or Honors? J2 is dragon, already counted. F4 is wind, if I make a pair of F4, that's not a pung. But if I get another dragon pung (J1) or wind pung, more points.\n\nBut I need to form two melds from my hand. Possible melds:\n- Chow from T tiles: e.g., T2, T3, T4 or T3, T4, T5. Need T4.\n- Pair: could be T3 pair (already have two), or pair F4, or pair J1, etc.\n\nIf I keep T3 pair, then I need two chows. With T2, T5, T8, it's hard. Maybe discard T8 and try for chows with T2, T3, T4 and T3, T4, T5? But overlapping T3 and T4.\n\nAlternatively, I could aim for a pair of honors and chows.\n\nLet's list the tiles needed:\nOption 1: Keep T3 pair. Then need two chows from remaining T2, T5, T8. T2 needs T1/T4, T5 needs T4/T6/T7, T8 needs T6/T7/T9. So need T4 and T6/T7/T9. Unshown T4:3, T6:4, T7:1, T9:1. Possible but not easy.\n\nOption 2: Keep F4 pair or J1 pair. Then need two chows and a pair from T tiles. For example, if I pair F4, then I need two chows from T tiles and a pair from T tiles (like T3 pair). So same issue.\n\nGiven the tile availability, perhaps keeping T3 pair is best because I already have two.\n\nNow, which discard improves my chances? I should discard the tile that is least useful for forming chows or pairs.\n\nB3 is completely isolated and not needed for All Types since I already have B suit represented with B3. But for All Types, I need at least one tile from each suit in the final hand. If I discard B3, I no longer have a B suit tile in my hand. But I still have B suit somewhere? My melds are W3 and J2, no B. So if I discard B3, I lose B suit, and then I would need to get another B suit tile later to satisfy All Types. That might be difficult. So to maintain All Types, I should keep B3 or get another B tile later.\n\nBut wait, All Types requires tiles from all three suits. Currently my hand has B3 (B suit), T tiles (T suit), and my meld has W suit. So if I discard B3, I no longer have B suit in my hand, but I could draw a B tile later to replace it. However, drawing a specific suit might reduce efficiency.\n\nMaybe it's better to discard a T tile that is less useful, like T8, and keep B3 to maintain All Types possibility.\n\nDiscard T8: T8 is high tile, not well connected. Keeping B3 gives me a B tile for All Types.\n\nDiscard T5: T5 is middle, could be useful for chows.\n\nDiscard T2: T2 is low, could be part of chow with T3.\n\nDiscard T3: breaks the pair.\n\nDiscard J1: loses a dragon, but might be okay.\n\nDiscard F4: loses a wind, but might be okay.\n\nGiven All Types is a valuable pattern (6 points) and I already have dragon pung (2 points), I need to preserve that combination. So I should keep at least one tile from each suit and one wind and one dragon. Currently: W suit from meld, T suit from hand, B suit from B3, wind from F4, dragon from J1 and J2 meld. So if I discard B3, I lose B suit, but I could still get a B tile later. However, that might be risky as B tiles are less in my hand.\n\nAlternatively, I could discard J1 because I already have a dragon (J2), and for All Types I need a dragon, which is satisfied by J2 meld. So J1 is extra. Discarding J1 simplifies hand.\n\nOr